SYSTEM DESIGN
The AR wayfinding system starts with a mobile application installed on the user's smartphone or tablet. The application serves as the user interface through which they interact with the AR wayfinding features. It can be a standalone app or integrated into an existing library app. The mob ile device's sensors, such as GPS, accelero meter, gyroscope, and compass, provide essential input to the AR wayfinding system. These sensors help determine the user's location, orientation, and movement, which are crucial fo r accurate positioning and align ment of the AR content. To enable precise indoor positioning, the AR wayfinding system emp loys location tracking technologies. These tech nologies help determine the user's position with in the lib rary and track their movements as they navigate.
High-precision GPS modules and inert ial sensors are examples of advanced sensor technologies that can be

integrated to greatly improve location tracking accuracy and responsiveness in augmented reality navigation. Furthermore, the integration of computer vision algorithms can improve the system's capacity to identify and comprehend its surroundings, allo wing for mo re accurate localization even in intricate metropolitan settings. This is achieved by utilising real-t ime environ mental data fro m onboard cameras. Moreover, future positions can be predicted by integrating machine learn ing algorith ms for predictive analysis based on past user motions, which enhances and anticipates the AR navigation experience.
Azimuth, or the horizontal angle of a user's direction with respect to a reference point, is often calcu lated in aug mented reality navigation utilising sensor data fro m the device. This entails making use of the magnetometers and accelero meters that are integrated within the device. While the accelero meter assists in tilt correction, the magnetometer determines the device's magnetic north direction. Algorith ms like sensor fusion or quaternion rotations can compute the azimuth precisely by merging these sensor readings. The computed azimuth is then utilised to ensure that virtual items appropriately match with the user's physical orientation, imp roving the precision of A R navigation by aligning AR overlays or annotations with the user's real-world view.
