The interface of the system contains three options such as search, about us and exit. It is pictured in fig.2. Selecting the search button will bring the user to a second interface in which there will be a search bar. User can enter their desired book’s name to attain the navigation bot which will guide the user to its position. After obtaining the book, user can exit by simply clicking on the exit button. In the process of creating an aug mented reality (AR) navigation system, the first step involves storing the GPS coordinates of the desired destination in a database. Simu ltaneously, the system obtains the GPS coordinates of the user's mobile phone, establishing the current location. The next crucial step is to retrieve the stored GPS coordinates of the destination fro m the database. Using this destination informat ion and the current GPS coordinates, the system calculates the direct ion, also known as the azimuth, in which the mobile device needs to be oriented for the user to face the desired destination. Once the azimuth is computed, the AR navigation system overlays Points of Interest (POI) onto the user's real-world v iew, indicating the correct direct ion in wh ich the mobile device should be pointed to reach the destination. These POI markers are rendered in the user's camera feed, providing a visual guide to the desired location. This interactive and intuitive approach enhances user navigation, ma king it easier fo r them to find their way by following the markers aligned with the calculated azimuth.
